{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1521087-rotes","id":1521087,"title":"Rotes","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-08-20T18:00:22-07:00","id":1521087,"title":"Rotes","content":"This thread is a consolidation of and expansion on several previous \u201crules hack\u201d threads that I've posted: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/3491-acolyte-magic\">Acolyte Magic</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1263470-spellbooks\">Spellbooks</a>, and <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/784502-composite-spheres-for-orphans\">Composite Spheres for Orphans</a>. The common thread tying these all together is a system that makes the ability to perform Effects available to people who otherwise wouldn't have access to them. <hr /><br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200bThe core of the system is the ability to learn a Rote without having the usual required Spheres or even Arete. You <i>do</i> need a teacher or spellbook to learn from (more on that later), and you need a Focus that's compatible with the Rote that's provided. And <i>that</i> means that you either need to be Awakened or you need to be an Extraordinary Citizen or an Acolyte \u2014 a Sleeper whose belief in magic is strong enough to let them use appropriate Artifacts or Devices. (For this purpose, I consider Sorcerers to also be Extraordinary Citizens or Acolytes; there's nothing preventing them from learning these Rotes.)<br /> <br /> Every Rote has a rating from 1 to 5, corresponding to the highest Sphere level that's normally required to perform it. In addition to the Effect that it has, the Rote specifies a Practice and Instruments that must be used whenever it is performed, as well as a prescribed way of using it. When it is performed, it works according to the usual rules for working an Effect, with the following changes:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>The Dice Pool is the greater of the Rote's rating or the performer's Arete. This allows people with insufficient Arete, even Sleepers, to perform the Rote. However, if you lack sufficient Arete for the Rote, performing it costs one Willpower.</li> <li>The Rote must be performed according to the prescribed method. In particular, you cannot Work Without Focus (M20 pp.566\u2013567) or discard any of its Instruments (\u201cGrowing Beyond the Tools\u201d, M20 p.567), even if you have discarded one of the Instruments for all other purposes. If your Arete is at least equal to the Rote's rating, you <i>do</i> have the option to Work With Unfamiliar Instruments (p.567); though you can never truly familiarize yourself with them until your Sphere ranks are sufficient to meet the Rote's usual requirements. Once you meet those requirements, the Rote has been Surpassed, and works like an Instrument that you've discarded: you're free to perform the Effect however you wish according to your own Focus; but if you perform it according to the prescribed method for the Rote, you get a \u20131 Magickal Difficulty Modifier.</li> </ol>If you want Rotes to be more useful, I would recommend combining these rules with the Rote rules found in <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/249171/Enlightened-Grimoire\" target=\"_blank\">Enlightened Grimoire</a>, an excellent supplement for characters using these rules.<br /> <br /> The Rote costs one Freebie Point per dot at character creation, or current rating\u00d72 to purchase it with experience points. Acolytes and Orphans (see below) get ten Freebie Points' worth of Rotes. Finally, Rotes acquired at character creation cannot exceed a rating of 3.<br /> <br /> There are four ways to learn a Rote:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>You can be taught the Rote by someone who already knows how to perform the Effect: either someone who knows the Rote, or someone who has the right Spheres to perform the Effect <i>without</i> the Rote.</li> <li>You can study a Spellbook. A Spellbook is like a cross between a Grimoire and an Artifact: anyone with an appropriate Focus who is in possession of a Spellbook can perform any of the Rote's inscribed within by following the prescribed method; and they can study it to justify spending experience points on the Rote.</li> <li>If you have sufficient Arete and knowledge of a similar Rote, you can improvise it at a +1 to +3 difficulty. The Storyteller is the final arbiter of what qualifies as \u201csimilar\u201d and of how much of a difficulty increase there is; and he is not obligated to take the Sphere requirements of the two Rotes into account. In fact, he's encouraged <i>not</i> to, and to judge \u201csimilarity\u201d solely on what the two Effects do. The difficulty increase should be at least +1 if the improvised Rote is the same level or lower than the base Rote, and at least +2 if it's a rank higher; Rotes more than one rank higher cannot be improvised. Successfully improving a Rote entitles you to spend experience to buy it.</li> <li>Wild Talent (M20 pp.527\u2013528). As with improvised Magick, a mage who manifests a Wild Talent is entitled to buy the Effect as a Rote with any experience he has on hand.</li> </ol><br /> As an optional rule, a mage who knows Rotes can use them to reduce the cost of purchasing the Spheres that would normally be required: if the purchase of a dot in a Sphere would result in any Rotes being Surpassed, the cost of that Sphere dot is reduced by one per Rote that's Surpassed, to a minimum of zero; but the Surpassed Rotes go away entirely, and neither grant a \u20131 difficulty nor allow for Rote-style improv anymore. <hr />\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> There are three main character types I have in mind for these rules:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li><i>Acolytes</i>. Here, this serves as a sort of \u201cMagick for beginners\u201d, an alternative to the Hedge Magic of <b>Sorcerer</b>. You can include both in your game, if you wish; you can even have a single character <i>have</i> both. But given just how much overlap there is between the two, you might want to pick just one or the other. Acolytes don't have Arete, so they must spend a point of Willpower to perform <i>any</i> of their Rotes; and because you must have Arete equal to or greater than your Rote in order to improvise with it, Acolytes cannot improvise.</li> <li><i>Regular mages with a trick or two up their sleeves</i>. This character concept relies primarily on her Spheres for casting, but has learned a handful of Rotes for things that she doesn't (yet?) have enough Spheres to perform otherwise, either directly or through other Rotes that she knows.</li> <li><i>Orphans</i>: mages who have had no exposure to Awakened society, and who know nothing of the Spheres. Their <i>entire</i> Magickal know-how comes from their Arete, whatever Rotes they've been able to find or improvise, and whatever their Focus happens to be. In particular, this is Magick without Spheres \u2014 but <i>not</i> without improv.</li> </ol>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> 